## 2. What is never an experiment

This list is what makes the permission safe to grant. **It is a starting point, and its provenance
is declared**: from the "Escalation" section of `github-plan-build/SKILL.md` it takes the rows that
are **boundaries of the permission** — not the ones that are gates of the delivery loop — **and
adds one of its own**, the last, which that list does not have. It is neither a copy nor a strict
subset: the two lists answer different questions, and forcing them to be identical is what pulls
the agent away from the agreement.

- Production writes, deploys, and any destructive or irreversible action.
- Real outreach to real recipients.
- Working around a missing credential or permission instead of asking for it.
- Changes touching authentication, secrets, or people's data. *(This one is not from the escalation
  list: the delivery loop does not stop for them, it requires `/security-review` as a mandatory
  gate and never proceeds on red. That leaves them **reviewed**, not permitted — if your team also
  wants them outside the permission, say so in section 1.)*
- <!-- TODO: whatever this team adds. Delete what does not apply, but say why. -->

**What is deliberately NOT copied from the escalation list**, because these are loop gates and not
boundaries of the permission: an ambiguous CI failure and a non-converging fix loop — both fire on
the state of the pipeline, not on what is allowed to be attempted — and a product-judgment call
with no source of truth, which is also **reversible**: what fails there is the plan, not
production. That the agent does not decide product for you is agreed by writing section 1's scope
well.

## 3. What happens when it goes wrong

<!-- TODO: what is expected of whoever tried, what is expected of the team, and what will NOT
     happen. -->

**The clause that decides whether this is real: the marker stays.**

The `Asistido-por-IA` trailer on a pull request that went badly is **not removed, not omitted, and
not up for discussion**. It is the only way the permission means anything: if the marker disappears
from the failed attempts, the record measures **successes only**, and this team will have learned
exactly what this file exists to prevent.

> **And it is checkable.** Compare the marker rate on reverted or hotfixed pull requests against
> the overall rate. If they differ, the agreement is written and not being kept — which is rather
> more useful than the number alone.

